# Do not leave scratch

This machine is not a dump. Disk is finite. A file that the task did not name is scratch. Delete it before the turn ends. That includes paths outside the repo.

Do not create, and do not leave, any of these:

- A git worktree
- A second checkout of this repo
- A throwaway crate, a downloaded tool, or its build tree
- A profile, a trace, or a `target/` directory that the task did not ask to keep
- A draft Issue or PR body saved as a file

The checkout already open is where the edit goes. `.dev/reviews/` is the only path under `.dev/` that stays. `docs/verification.md` names `.dev/perf/` for point files of a measurement the user asked to run. Profiles and anything else beside those files are removed in that same turn.

A change that belongs in the repo is not finished as a local edit. It is finished only when its Issue has an open pull request (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`).

A script that writes scratch stops at its first failed command (`set -e` in bash, `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` in PowerShell) and names its targets by absolute path. Do not rely on a `cd` earlier in an `&&` chain: when a command before it fails, the lines after the chain run in the repository. In #259 that overwrote `Cargo.toml` and left two files in the checkout.

```text
# BAD — a worktree, a timing crate, and a profile still on disk after the reply
git worktree add .dev/wt-133
.dev/time_main/target/
.dev/perf/profile/

# GOOD — the edit is in the open checkout; the measurement files are gone before the reply
```
